# Line endings
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on the development machines here, so Git checks
# every text file out with CRLF on Windows. For most of the tree that is
# harmless. For an executable script that ALSO carries a shebang it is not:
#
#   SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token
#
# ...raised by Vitest, with no file and no line number, when a test imports such
# a module. `scripts/unconditional-policy-guard.mjs` is imported by
# `src/lib/security/__tests__/unconditional-policy-guard.test.ts`, and that is
# exactly how it failed.
#
# The cause is the interaction, not either half. Vite neutralises a shebang with
#
#     result.code.replace(/^#!.*/, s => ' '.repeat(s.length))
#
# and in a JavaScript regular expression `.` matches anything EXCEPT a line
# terminator — `\r` included. With LF the whole `#!...` line is blanked. With
# CRLF the match stops short and the transformed module is no longer valid
# input. Plain `node` is unaffected, because its own loader strips the shebang
# before parsing, which is why the CLI kept working while the test could not
# even load. Neither CRLF alone nor a shebang alone reproduces it.
#
# Pinning LF here fixes the whole class rather than one file: ~30 scripts in
# this repository start with a shebang, and any of them could be imported by a
# test tomorrow. It also makes the checkout identical on Windows, macOS, Linux
# and CI, so this cannot come back as "works on my machine".
#
# Deliberately narrow. A repo-wide `* text=auto eol=lf` would renormalise
# thousands of files for one defect. These blobs are already stored LF in Git
# (that is what autocrlf does), so this changes only what lands in the working
# tree — no content diff, no churn.

*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# TypeScript, for the same reason one layer up: 87 test suites read their own
# source and anchor on multi-line string literals containing `\n` -- 1122 such
# anchors. Checked out CRLF, every one of them silently misses, `indexOf`
# returns -1, and the slice under assertion is empty. That is how
# `pilot-executed-trade.test.tsx` began failing: not because the test or the
# code changed, but because Git rewrote `SimulationPage.tsx` on checkout.
#
# Nothing here expects CRLF, and no tracked blob contains one, so this is the
# same no-content-diff change as the block above -- it only settles what lands
# in the working tree, identically on every machine and in CI.

*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
